# What NOT to upload to Vercel.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. The first CLI deploy of this project reported "Uploading (0.0B/426.9MB)"
# and failed on transport. The tracked tree is 24MB — `git ls-files | du -ch` — so roughly
# 400MB of it was build output and dependency trees the platform reinstalls and rebuilds
# anyway. A 426MB upload is slow, fragile, and hides what is actually being shipped.
#
# EVERY DIRECTORY PATTERN IS ANCHORED WITH A LEADING SLASH, and that is load-bearing.
# .vercelignore matches at any depth, exactly like .gitignore. The first version of this file
# wrote `server`, `supabase` and `docs` unanchored, which also matched:
#
#     lib/server/*        the entire server-side library — the build failed with
#                         "Can't resolve '@/lib/server/admin'"
#     lib/supabase.ts     the Supabase client
#     app/docs/page.tsx   a live route, which would have vanished from the build
#
# The first of those failed loudly. The third would not have: the route would simply have
# stopped existing, and nothing in the deploy output would have said so.
#
# Nothing here changes what the application DOES. The Next build reads app/, components/,
# lib/, public/ and the manifests; none of the paths below is imported by any of them.

# Reinstalled and rebuilt by the platform. Deliberately unanchored: node_modules must match at
# every depth, which is what picks up server/node_modules and server/bot/node_modules.
node_modules
.next
tsconfig.tsbuildinfo

# The Discord listener and the trading worker. Deployed separately, and — with one deliberate
# exception below — imported by nothing in the Next build. NOT lib/server, which the build needs.
#
# THE EXCEPTION. `app/api/cron/call-performance` drives `server/engine/performance.js`, the
# call-price scanner. That module used to run only inside the worker, and the worker has no
# host: the Railway trial expired and its deployments were torn down, leaving 1,780 of 1,781
# journalled calls unpriced and the marketplace with nothing to show.
#
# The obvious alternatives are both wrong here. Moving the engine to `lib/` breaks the worker,
# which deploys with `rootDir: server` and cannot see `lib/` at runtime — the same constraint
# that makes the worker mirror PLATFORM_FEE_BPS rather than import it. Copying the engine into
# the route gives two definitions of "what counts as a measured call", which is the defect
# class this codebase keeps finding. So one file crosses the boundary, and only that one.
#
# The four lines below are gitignore semantics: a file cannot be re-included once its parent
# directory is excluded, so each level is opened with `/*` before the exception is named.
/server/*
!/server/engine
#
# WIDENED 2026-08-11 from the single performance.js file to the whole engine directory.
#
# `app/api/worker/tick` runs the execution engine — exits and entries — from Vercel, because
# the worker has no host: Railway's trial expired and removed every deployment, and a new host
# needs an account this environment does not have. The engine is dependency-injected and
# provider-agnostic, so the same modules the worker drives run unchanged behind an HTTP route.
#
# Only `engine/` crosses. `server/worker.js`, `server/bot/` and `server/test/` stay excluded:
# the worker's own process entry point must not be bundled into the app, and the listener is a
# separate program with its own package.json.
#
# The alternative was copying the exit logic into the route, which would give two definitions
# of when a stop loss fires. That is the defect class this codebase keeps finding, and it is
# not one to repeat on the code that sells a user's position.

# Database migrations and rollback scripts. Applied through the Supabase MCP connection, never
# read at request time. NOT lib/supabase.ts.
/supabase

# Verification tooling and its evidence. docs/ai/evidence holds several megabytes of
# screenshots that no route serves. NOT app/docs, which is a route.
/scripts
/docs

# Local-only.
/.git
/.github
/.claude
/.agents
/.references
*.log
.DS_Store
